  "step": [
    {
      "@type": "HowToStep",
      "position": 1,
      "name": "Step 1",
      "text": "Electrical rough-in is a critical stage in any remodel, and preventing conflicts here saves a ton of headaches later on. Here's a simple, step-by-step approach to make sure your electrical work goes smoothly:"
    },
    {
      "@type": "HowToStep",
      "position": 2,
      "name": "Step 2",
      "text": "Step 1: Overlay Drawings Early. Before any work begins, have your general contractor overlay the electrical, plumbing, and HVAC drawings. This helps identify potential clash zones where different systems might compete for space, especially in ceilings or tight wall cavities."
    },
    {
      "@type": "HowToStep",
      "position": 3,
      "name": "Step 3",
      "text": "Step 2: Agree on Routing and Priority. Once potential conflicts are identified, get the trades together to agree on the best routing for each system. Sometimes, one system might need priority in a congested area. This avoids costly rework."
    },
    {
      "@type": "HowToStep",
      "position": 4,
      "name": "Step 4",
      "text": "Step 3: Hold Pre-Construction and Weekly Meetings. Communication is key! A pre-construction meeting sets the stage, and weekly check-ins keep everyone on track. This is where everyone can voice concerns and coordinate their next steps."
    },
    {
      "@type": "HowToStep",
      "position": 5,
      "name": "Step 5",
      "text": "Step 4: Verify Rough-In Before Closing Walls. Before any drywall goes up or ceilings are closed, conduct a thorough rough-in inspection. This is your last chance to ensure everything is correctly installed, routed, and ready for the next phase. Catching mistakes now is far cheaper than after everything is covered up. When you have multiple trades and complex loads, a professional like HH Remodeling LLC makes sure these steps are followed diligently, protecting your investment."
    }
  ],
